// @korulang/unikraft-allocpool — Unikraft's fixed-size object pool
// (`ukallocpool`), lifted.
//
//   import unikraft/allocpool
//
// This is NOT a naive wrap. Reachability was never the open question here:
// `lib/ukallocpool` has 12 allowlist lines, 12 real definitions, and ZERO
// `static inline`s in its public header. Everything links. The open question was
// PROVENANCE, and that is what this module is about.
//
// WHAT THIS IS. `ukallocpool` hands out fixed-size objects from one contiguous
// slab, LIFO, with no per-object metadata. It has TWO CONSTRUCTORS and ONE
// DESTRUCTOR, and the destructor is legal for exactly one of the two:
//
//     uk_allocpool_alloc(parent, …)  -> a pool that owns its slab
//     uk_allocpool_init(base, len, …) -> a pool placed in memory YOU own
//     uk_allocpool_free(p)            -> legal ONLY for the first
//
// The C says so in an assertion and in a comment above it (pool.c:379-383):
//
//     /* If we do not have a parent, this pool was created with
//      * uk_allocpool_init(). Such a pool cannot be free'd with
//      * this function since we are not the owner of the allocation */
//     UK_ASSERT(p->parent);
//
// `p->parent` is private, set only by `uk_allocpool_alloc` (pool.c:373), and
// `UK_ASSERT` is `do {} while(0)` in every image this shelf builds. So in a
// shipped unikernel the rule is not merely unchecked — it is UNCHECKABLE from
// the outside, because the field that decides it is not reachable. Hand a
// `uk_allocpool_init` pool to `uk_allocpool_free` and it calls `uk_free` on an
// address the byte allocator never handed out.
//
// THE SECOND RULE, three lines below it (pool.c:385-386):
//
//     /* Make sure we got all objects back */
//     UK_ASSERT(p->free_obj_count == p->obj_count);
//
// Drain before free. Also a counter comparison, also compiled out.
//
// ── AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT REFRAMES BOTH ─────────────────────────────────
//
// `uk_allocpool_free` CANNOT RUN. Four lines below the second assertion, before
// it reaches `uk_free`, sits pool.c:390:
//
//     UK_CRASH("Unregistering from `lib/ukalloc` not implemented.\n");
//
// `UK_CRASH` is NOT gated on `CONFIG_LIBUKDEBUG_ENABLE_ASSERT` — read
// `lib/ukdebug/include/uk/crash.h:18-22`: it is `uk_pr_crit` followed by
// `uk_crash_trigger()`, unconditionally, in every build. So the library's only
// destructor halts the machine in debug AND in release, and BOTH of the rules
// above are unreachable in the C: you die before you can violate either.
//
// WHY the author did that is legible from the FIXME immediately above it
// (pool.c:388) and from `uk_alloc_init_malloc` (alloc_impl.h:227), which every
// pool constructor runs: **a pool REGISTERS ITSELF into `_uk_alloc_head`'s
// chain, and `lib/ukalloc` has no unregister.** Freeing the slab would leave a
// live list node pointing into freed memory, and `uk_alloc_availmem_total`
// walks that chain. Crashing is the honest choice available in C. It is also
// why this lift's `keep` is not a convenience — see below.
//
// This is measured, not read off the shipped console: tests/boot_free_crashes.kz
// calls `free` on an owned pool and the console prints the crash. It is the one
// artifact in this shelf whose success criterion is that the machine stops.
//
// ── THE SHAPE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//     create     mints  *Pool<owned!>                  uk_allocpool_alloc
//     host       mints  *Pool<hosted!>                 uk_allocpool_init
//     take       (pool bare)  mints *Obj<fresh!>       uk_allocpool_take
//     write      needs  *Obj<!fresh|!used>  mints <used!>   (no C call)
//     read       needs  *Obj<!used>         mints <used!>   (no C call)
//     untouched  needs  *Obj<!fresh>        mints <used!>   (NO C CALL — escape)
//     give       needs  *Obj<!used>                     uk_allocpool_return
//     census     (pool bare)                            availcount/maxcount/objlen
//     free       needs  *Pool<!owned>                   uk_allocpool_free  ← crashes
//     keep       needs  *Pool<!owned|!hosted>           (NO C CALL — the truth)
//
// ── THE INSTRUMENT, AND WHY IT IS PHANTOM STATES AND NOT TWO HANDLE TYPES ──
//
// The brief leaves this to the contestant, so: **one handle type, two phantom
// states, and every operational tor takes the pool through a BARE parameter.**
//
// The argument is mechanical, not aesthetic. `take`, `give`, `write`, `read`
// and `census` are provenance-BLIND — where a pool came from has no bearing on
// whether you may take an object from it. A bare `pool: *Pool` parameter neither
// reads nor spends the phantom state (measured; see below), so ONE set of
// operational tors serves both provenances at zero duplication. Two handle types
// would have forced `take`/`give`/`write`/`read`/`census` to exist twice — ten
// tors where five do — to encode a distinction none of the ten consults.
//
// **Put the distinction where it is consulted.** It is consulted in exactly one
// place, `free`, and a phantom state is precisely a fact carried to the site
// that consults it.
//
// The counter-argument, stated because it is the better half of the case for
// types: a phantom state models "what may I do next" and normally TRANSITIONS,
// and provenance never does — a pool's origin is fixed at birth, which is what a
// type is for. That is true, and it is why the two states here are terminal:
// nothing in this module transitions `owned` to `hosted` or back. What decided
// it is that the alternative was not "a type instead of a state" but "a type
// instead of a state, plus a duplicate of every verb", and pillar 1 is developer
// experience.
//
// This was measured before it was chosen, not after. A bare `*Pool` parameter
// against a `<owned!>` binding compiles, threads the obligation through
// arbitrarily many `take`/`give` pairs, and still refuses at the end if the pool
// is dropped. The probe is `tests/pool_threads_through_operations.kz`.
//
// ── WHAT THE COMPILER REFUSES, AND WHAT ARGUMENT MAKES IT REFUSE ───────────
//
//   1. THE PAIRING. `free` accepts `*Pool<!owned>`. Only `create` mints
//      `<owned!>`. So
//
//          host(…) | pool p |> … |> free(p)
//
//      is `error[KORU030]: Phantom state mismatch: expected
//      'unikraft.allocpool:owned' but got 'unikraft.allocpool:hosted!'`.
//      pool.c:383, lifted. tests/negative_free_hosted_pool.kz.
//
//   2. DRAIN BEFORE FREE, and it falls out of two facts rather than a counter.
//      `give` is a TWO-parameter disposer, so auto-discharge can never elect it;
//      an object that is not explicitly returned is always `KORU030 … obligation
//      <fresh!>/<used!> was not discharged`. And `free`/`keep` CONSUME the pool,
//      so a `give` after either is `Use-after-discharge: binding 'p' …`.
//      Together: every object taken must be returned, and it must be returned
//      before the pool ends. pool.c:386, lifted — with no counter, in a build
//      where the C has no counter either. tests/negative_object_outstanding.kz.
//
//   3. THE ASYMMETRY (pillar 4), placed on the OBJECT. `give` accepts only
//      `<!used>`, and `<used!>` is reachable only through `write`, `read` or the
//      named escape `untouched`. `take` immediately followed by `give` does not
//      compile. tests/negative_give_without_use.kz.
//
// ── WHERE PILLAR 4 IS REFUSED, AND WHY ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// **The POOL gets no use-before-finalize gate.** `create(…) |> keep(…)` — a pool
// nothing was ever taken from — compiles. That is a deliberate refusal and the
// reason is that the pool has TWO orthogonal facts and one state axis: where it
// came from (permanent, penalty = `uk_free` on an address the allocator never
// issued) and whether it was ever used (monotone, penalty = a few KiB reserved
// and not used). One axis, two candidates, and it goes to the one whose
// violation corrupts the heap. Ratcheting the pool would have required `take` to
// spend and re-mint the pool state, which is the duplication argued away above.
//
// `unikraft/vmem` made the same shape of call — pillar 4 on the committed half,
// refused on the reservation half — for the same kind of reason.
//
// ── WHY `keep` IS NOT A CONVENIENCE ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `keep` runs no C call and mints nothing. It is how a pool's obligation is
// discharged when the pool outlives the program, which in Unikraft is EVERY
// POOL, because `uk_allocpool_free` crashes and there is no unregister. It is
// also what gives `<owned>` two disposals, and that is load-bearing: with two,
// dropping a pool is
//
//     error[KORU030]: Resource 'p' <owned!> has multiple discharge options:
//                     keep, free. Discharge explicitly.
//
// With one it would be silently auto-inserted. This is the dividend
// `unikraft/alloc` recorded — a named escape buys a DIAGNOSTIC, not just a grep
// — applied on purpose rather than discovered.
//
//     grep -r "allocpool:keep"   ->   "which pools live for the life of the machine"
//
// `<hosted>` has only `keep`, so a dropped hosted pool IS auto-inserted rather
// than reported. Stated because it is an asymmetry in the guarantee, not in the
// prose: there is no honest second disposal for a hosted pool, and inventing one
// to buy a diagnostic would be inventing a C call that does not exist.
//
// ── DEDUP AGAINST `unikraft/alloc` AND `unikraft/pages` ────────────────────
//
// Both are allocators; so is this. The overlap is real and is confined to the
// object contents axis, which this module deliberately does NOT re-argue:
// `fresh`/`used` here is the same instrument as `alloc`'s `raw`/`live` and
// `pages`' `blank`/`filled`, for the same reason, and `untouched` is the same
// named escape with the same spelling because a third word for one idea is a
// worse outcome than a repeated one. That axis is not this lift's contribution
// and is not claimed as one.
//
// What is NOT in either neighbour:
//
//   * **Two constructors and one destructor.** `ukalloc` has one of each;
//     `uk_palloc`/`uk_pfree` likewise. Neither has a provenance rule, so
//     neither has anything resembling `free`'s `<!owned>` gate.
//   * **Drain-before-destroy.** A `*Block` and a `*Pages` region have no
//     sub-resources. A pool's objects are sub-resources of the pool, and the
//     rule that they must all be back before it ends has no analogue there.
//   * **No handle header, and that is a deliberate divergence.** `alloc` and
//     `pages` both put a header struct in front of the caller's region.
//     A POOL'S CONTRACT IS ABOUT EXACT SIZES — N objects of exactly L bytes,
//     that is the whole reason to use one — so silently spending
//     `alignUp(16, align)` bytes of every object on a lift's bookkeeping would
//     be the lift lying about the machine. Instead `write`, `read` and `give`
//     take the pool as a second argument and read the length back with
//     `uk_allocpool_objlen`, one exported call, no bytes. Ceremony at the call
//     site, zero cost in the image.
//   * **`Pool` and `Obj` are `opaque`.** No struct is mirrored, no offset is
//     claimed, nothing is transcribed. `pages`, `sched` and `lock` each had to
//     pay a mirror proof; this one has nothing to prove because every fact it
//     needs has an exported accessor.
//
// ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT BOUND ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   * **`uk_allocpool2ukalloc`.** It hands out a `struct uk_alloc *` so the pool
//     can be driven through `uk_malloc`/`uk_free`. That converts every object
//     into an untracked `void *` with no obligation and no state — the exact
//     hole this module exists to close — and it re-opens `pool_malloc`'s
//     `UK_ASSERT(size <= p->obj_len)` (pool.c:169), a caller rule that the
//     typed surface does not have because `write` is bounded by `objlen`.
//     Binding it would be handing back the footgun with a bow on it.
//   * **`uk_allocpool_take_batch` / `uk_allocpool_return_batch`.** One call
//     yielding N independently-owed objects into a C array is not a shape a
//     phantom obligation can carry — the compiler would have to track an
//     array of obligations whose length is a runtime value. A `batch` that
//     handed back one handle for N objects would be a different resource with
//     a different rule, honestly modelled and not equivalent to the C. Not
//     attempted, and no assertion of theirs is counted as retired
//     (pool.c:207-208, :235-236).
//   * **`uk_allocpool_reqmem` as a public tor.** It is a sizing helper whose
//     only consumer is a constructor, and both constructors here call it
//     internally. Exposing it would invite a caller to compute a length and
//     then pass a different one.
//
// ── ONE HONEST LIMIT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `give(pool, obj)` does NOT prove the object came from THAT pool. With two
// pools in scope, crossing them is spellable, and it is exactly what
// `_prepend_free_obj`'s `UK_ASSERT(p->free_obj_count < p->obj_count)` and
// `UK_ASSERT(IS_ALIGNED(obj, p->obj_align))` (pool.c:114, :115) exist to catch.
// Proving it needs an object type parameterised by its pool, which this
// language does not have for phantom handles. Those two assertions are counted
// as NOT RETIRED in README.md, and this paragraph is why.

// ============================================================================
// UNIKRAFT C ABI
// ============================================================================
//
// Linkability, settled the way the linker settles it, and it is the easy case:
// `lib/ukallocpool/exportsyms.uk` EXISTS — case 1 — with **12 lines**. All 12
// name a real, non-static definition in `pool.c` (checked by grep, so case 4
// is zero). The public header contains **zero `static inline`s**, so case 5 is
// zero and case 3 does not arise at all. `links = 12`, and this module binds 9
// of them; the other 3 are refused above with reasons.
//
// No token pasting anywhere in the library, so the case-4 inverse trap does not
// apply either. `lib/ukallocpool` is BYTE-IDENTICAL between our fork at
// `3fdffba8` and Unikraft stable 0.21.0 "Ijiraq" — `pool.c` and `exportsyms.uk`
// both diffed clean — so the version trap on the shelf does not apply here and
// what is measured against the fork is what the `version: stable` Kraftfile
// builds.

// No `const std` here on purpose. `unikraft/index.kz` (line 208) already
// declares one, and it is auto-pulled into scope alongside this submodule —
// so a second declaration here is an `ambiguous reference` in the emitted Zig
// the moment any tor referencing `std.*` is actually compiled. Zig lexical
// scoping already makes the enclosing declaration visible; this one buys
// nothing and detonates on first use. Repro: ai/toolchain-repros/
// D_unikraft_submodule_std_ambiguous.kz plus its control.

const UkAlloc = opaque {};

// `struct uk_allocpool` is opaque in the public header (allocpool.h:47) and
// stays opaque here. Every fact this lift needs — free count, capacity, object
// length — has an exported accessor, so there is nothing to mirror and no
// offset to claim.
const Pool = opaque {};

// One object out of the pool. A bare address into the slab: the pool keeps no
// per-object metadata and neither does this lift. See the header note on why
// there is no handle header.
const Obj = opaque {};

// The default allocator. `uk_alloc_get_default()` is `static inline`
// (ukalloc alloc.h:136-139) and returns exactly this exported global, so
// reading the variable is the same load the inline performs. NULL until ukboot
// registers an allocator.
extern var _uk_alloc_head: ?*UkAlloc;

extern fn uk_allocpool_reqmem(obj_count: c_uint, obj_len: usize, obj_align: usize) usize;
extern fn uk_allocpool_alloc(parent: *UkAlloc, obj_count: c_uint, obj_len: usize, obj_align: usize) ?*Pool;
extern fn uk_allocpool_init(base: *anyopaque, len: usize, obj_len: usize, obj_align: usize) ?*Pool;
extern fn uk_allocpool_free(p: *Pool) void;
extern fn uk_allocpool_take(p: *Pool) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn uk_allocpool_return(p: *Pool, obj: *anyopaque) void;
extern fn uk_allocpool_availcount(p: *Pool) c_uint;
extern fn uk_allocpool_maxcount(p: *Pool) c_uint;
extern fn uk_allocpool_objlen(p: *Pool) usize;

// ============================================================================
// THE HOSTED SLAB
// ============================================================================
//
// `uk_allocpool_init` places a pool in memory the CALLER owns, which is the
// whole point of its existing beside `uk_allocpool_alloc`. In a unikernel that
// memory is characteristically not heap memory — it is a statically reserved
// region, sized at build time, present before the allocator is. This module
// provides exactly that, in its own `.bss`, because it is the case the C's
// second constructor is FOR and because it makes the provenance rule
// demonstrable inside one module with no cross-module handle passing.
//
// THE SIZE IS A DECLARED CONSTANT, not a computed one, and it is stated rather
// than buried: 8 KiB. `host` refuses any request `uk_allocpool_reqmem` sizes
// above it, naming both numbers. There is no growth path and no fallback to the
// heap — that would make `host` and `create` the same tor wearing two names,
// which would make the provenance rule fiction.
const ARENA_LEN: usize = 8192;

var arena: [ARENA_LEN]u8 align(16) = undefined;

// The arena may be hosted ONCE per boot, and this flag is not bookkeeping
// hygiene — it prevents a hang.
//
// `uk_allocpool_init` ends in `uk_alloc_init_malloc` (alloc_impl.h:206-228),
// whose last statement is `uk_alloc_register(a)`. `uk_alloc_register`
// (ukalloc alloc.c:57-71) walks to the tail of `_uk_alloc_head`'s chain and
// appends. Re-initialising a pool AT THE SAME ADDRESS therefore walks to a tail
// that IS the node being appended, and writes `this->next = a` with `this == a`
// — a self-loop. `uk_alloc_availmem_total` walks that chain
// (ukalloc alloc.c:594-603) and would never return.
//
// Derived by reading `uk_alloc_register` and `uk_alloc_init_malloc`, NOT
// measured on a booted image: demonstrating a hang is not a console line. It is
// refused rather than risked, and the refusal says which mechanism it is
// avoiding.
var arena_hosted: bool = false;

// ============================================================================
// CONSTRUCT
// ============================================================================

// `uk_allocpool_alloc` — a pool whose slab comes from the default allocator, so
// the pool owns it. Mints `<owned!>`, the ONE state `free` accepts.
//
// Three arms, not two, and for the reason `unikraft/alloc:default` splits its
// own: `uk_allocpool_alloc` answers "ukboot never registered an allocator" and
// "the allocator could not find the bytes" with the same NULL, and they are
// different problems with different fixes. Conflating them would be a silent
// fallback in the shelf's sense — a report that reads like the real one.
//
// The emitted call is one `uk_allocpool_alloc`. The `uk_allocpool_reqmem` +
// `uk_malloc` + `uk_allocpool_init` sequence happens inside it (pool.c:362-374),
// which is where it belongs; this lift does not re-implement it.
//
// `objects`, `bytes` and `align` are checked here BEFORE the call, because
// `uk_allocpool_reqmem` and `uk_allocpool_init` both open with
// `UK_ASSERT(POWER_OF_2(obj_align))` (pool.c:264, :298) and that assertion is
// compiled out. A non-power-of-two alignment reaches `ALIGN_UP` as a mask and
// silently produces a pool with garbage geometry. This is the one place the lift
// keeps a check the C only asserts, rather than making it unspellable.
~pub tor create { objects: u32, bytes: u64, alignment: u64 }
| pool *Pool<owned!>
| refused string
| unavailable string

~proc create|zig {
    const a = _uk_alloc_head orelse {
        return .{ .unavailable = "no allocator registered: ukboot has not run, or CONFIG_LIBUKALLOC is off" };
    };
    if (objects == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "a pool of zero objects: uk_allocpool_init reports obj_count == 0 as ENOSPC (pool.c:323) and there would be nothing to take" };
    }
    if (bytes == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "a zero-byte object: uk_allocpool_init would silently widen it to sizeof(struct uk_list_head) (pool.c:306) and the pool would not have the geometry that was asked for" };
    }
    if (alignment == 0 or (alignment & (alignment - 1)) != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "obj_align must be a power of two: pool.c:264 and pool.c:298 assert it and the assertion is compiled out in this image, after which ALIGN_UP is a mask over a non-mask" };
    }
    const n: c_uint = @intCast(objects);
    const len: usize = @intCast(bytes);
    const al: usize = @intCast(alignment);
    const p = uk_allocpool_alloc(a, n, len, al) orelse {
        return .{ .refused = "uk_allocpool_alloc returned NULL: the parent allocator could not supply the slab, or the geometry does not fit one" };
    };
    return .{ .pool = p };
}

// `uk_allocpool_init` — a pool placed in this program's static arena. Mints
// `<hosted!>`, which `free` does not accept and never will.
//
// The slab is NOT this pool's to release, which is precisely what pool.c:383
// says and precisely what the phantom state carries. There is no `dissolve`
// tor and its absence is deliberate: the arena cannot be re-hosted (see
// `arena_hosted`), the registration cannot be undone (pool.c:388's FIXME), so
// there is nothing a release could honestly do. `keep` is the disposal.
//
// The length handed to `uk_allocpool_init` is `uk_allocpool_reqmem`'s answer
// and not `ARENA_LEN`, so a hosted pool has the geometry that was asked for
// rather than however many objects happen to fit the arena. The unused tail
// stays unused.
~pub tor host { objects: u32, bytes: u64, alignment: u64 }
| pool *Pool<hosted!>
| refused string
| busy string

~proc host|zig {
    if (arena_hosted) {
        return .{ .busy = "the static arena already holds a pool: uk_allocpool_init registers the pool with uk_alloc_register (alloc_impl.h:227), and re-registering the same address makes _uk_alloc_head's chain self-loop, after which uk_alloc_availmem_total never returns" };
    }
    if (objects == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "a pool of zero objects: uk_allocpool_init reports obj_count == 0 as ENOSPC (pool.c:323) and there would be nothing to take" };
    }
    if (bytes == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "a zero-byte object: uk_allocpool_init would silently widen it to sizeof(struct uk_list_head) (pool.c:306) and the pool would not have the geometry that was asked for" };
    }
    if (alignment == 0 or (alignment & (alignment - 1)) != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = "obj_align must be a power of two: pool.c:264 and pool.c:298 assert it and the assertion is compiled out in this image, after which ALIGN_UP is a mask over a non-mask" };
    }
    const n: c_uint = @intCast(objects);
    const len: usize = @intCast(bytes);
    const al: usize = @intCast(alignment);
    const need = uk_allocpool_reqmem(n, len, al);
    if (need > ARENA_LEN) {
        return .{ .refused = "the requested pool does not fit this module's static arena; uk_allocpool_reqmem asked for more than the 8192 bytes reserved, and there is no growth path because a hosted pool that silently moved to the heap would be a pool this module reported the wrong provenance for" };
    }
    const p = uk_allocpool_init(@ptrCast(&arena), need, len, al) orelse {
        return .{ .refused = "uk_allocpool_init returned NULL over the static arena: the geometry does not fit the length uk_allocpool_reqmem computed for it" };
    };
    arena_hosted = true;
    return .{ .pool = p };
}

// ============================================================================
// READ THE POOL
// ============================================================================

// `uk_allocpool_availcount`, `uk_allocpool_maxcount` and `uk_allocpool_objlen`,
// as one reading. Three exported calls, no state taken and none minted: knowing
// how full a pool is constrains nothing about what you may do next.
//
// This is the bracket instrument. Two censuses around a program's whole use of
// a pool, with `free` equal at both ends, are the RUN-TIME evidence that every
// object came back — which is a different claim from the compile-time one, and
// the boot test makes both.
//
// `objlen` is the ALIGNED object length (`obj_alen`, pool.c:313), which is what
// `write` and `read` are bounded by and may be larger than the `bytes` a
// constructor was given. Reported as what it is rather than as what was asked
// for, because the difference is exactly the slack a caller may legally use.
~pub tor census { pool: *Pool } -> { free: u32, capacity: u32, objlen: u64 }

~proc census|zig {
    return .{
        .free = @intCast(uk_allocpool_availcount(pool)),
        .capacity = @intCast(uk_allocpool_maxcount(pool)),
        .objlen = @intCast(uk_allocpool_objlen(pool)),
    };
}

// ============================================================================
// TAKE AND GIVE BACK
// ============================================================================

// `uk_allocpool_take`. Mints `<fresh!>`.
//
// The pool parameter is BARE — no state read, no state spent. That is the whole
// reason one handle type carries both provenances; see the header note.
//
// AN EXHAUSTED POOL NEVER BECOMES AN OBJECT. `uk_allocpool_take` returns NULL
// with `errno = ENOMEM` (pool.c:132-134) and the header documents it
// (allocpool.h:163). The hazard the C leaves open is the next line of the
// caller's program: `uk_allocpool_return(p, NULL)` walks into
// `_prepend_free_obj`, whose `UK_ASSERT(obj)` (pool.c:113) is compiled out, and
// links a list entry at address 0. Here NULL takes the `| empty` arm and no
// `*Obj` is minted, so there is no NULL to hand back.
//
// `<fresh>` is a real state and not ceremony: the pool is LIFO and does not
// clear objects, so a taken object's first `sizeof(struct uk_list_head)` bytes
// are the freelist links `_prepend_free_obj` wrote when it was last returned
// (pool.c:117-118). Reading a fresh object reads the allocator's own pointers.
~pub tor take { pool: *Pool }
| obj *Obj<fresh!>
| empty string

~proc take|zig {
    const raw = uk_allocpool_take(pool) orelse {
        return .{ .empty = "the pool has no free objects left; uk_allocpool_take returned NULL with errno ENOMEM" };
    };
    return .{ .obj = @ptrCast(raw) };
}

// Copy `bytes` into the object from offset 0 and mint `<used!>`. No C call —
// the pool hands out addresses, it does not touch contents.
//
// The bound is `uk_allocpool_objlen(pool)`, read at the call rather than stored
// in a header. That is the divergence from `unikraft/alloc` and
// `unikraft/pages` argued in the header note: a pool's contract is exact sizes,
// so no byte of an object belongs to this lift.
//
// A refusal hands the object back as `<fresh!>`. Nothing was copied, so nothing
// is provably initialised, and a refused write must not satisfy the asymmetry
// gate — otherwise take + an over-long write + give is a hole straight through
// it. Same call `unikraft/alloc:write` makes, same reason.
~pub tor write { pool: *Pool, obj: *Obj<!fresh|!used>, bytes: string }
| ok *Obj<used!>
| rejected { obj: *Obj<fresh!>, reason: string }

~proc write|zig {
    if (bytes.len == 0) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .obj = obj, .reason = "write of zero bytes" } };
    }
    const cap = uk_allocpool_objlen(pool);
    if (bytes.len > cap) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .obj = obj, .reason = "write is longer than the pool's object length" } };
    }
    const region: [*]u8 = @ptrCast(obj);
    for (0..bytes.len) |i| region[i] = bytes[i];
    return .{ .ok = obj };
}

// Borrow `len` bytes from offset `at`. `bytes` is a BORROW of the object's own
// memory — valid until the next `write` or `give`, never freed by the caller,
// never a second obligation. Same discipline as `alloc`'s and `gzip`'s views.
//
// Accepts ONLY `<!used>`. An object straight out of `take` is `<fresh!>` and
// cannot reach this tor, which is the freelist-pointer read described on `take`.
//
// The bound is the object length. Unlike `unikraft/alloc:read`, which is bounded
// by how much was written, there is no `used` counter here — there is nowhere to
// keep one without a header, and a header is what this module refuses to charge
// for. So `read` can return slack the caller did not write, and the state gate
// is what makes that honest: the object was written by SOMETHING in this
// program before it could be read at all. Stated as a limit in README.md.
~pub tor read { pool: *Pool, obj: *Obj<!used>, at: u64, len: u64 }
| view { obj: *Obj<used!>, bytes: string }
| rejected { obj: *Obj<used!>, reason: string }

~proc read|zig {
    if (len == 0) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .obj = obj, .reason = "read of zero bytes" } };
    }
    const from: usize = @intCast(at);
    const count: usize = @intCast(len);
    const end = from + count;
    const cap = uk_allocpool_objlen(pool);
    if (end < from or end > cap) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .obj = obj, .reason = "read runs past the pool's object length" } };
    }
    const region: [*]u8 = @ptrCast(obj);
    return .{ .view = .{ .obj = obj, .bytes = region[from..end] } };
}

// THE NAMED ESCAPE from the asymmetry gate, for objects.
//
// `give` accepts only `<!used>`, so an object taken and handed straight back
// does not compile. That is the gate — but taking an object to prove the pool
// has capacity, or to hold a slot so a later `take` cannot lose the race, is a
// legitimate thing to do with a pool, and this is how it says so out loud.
//
// Runs no C call. What it changes is what may be written next, and what grep
// can find:
//
//     grep -r "allocpool:untouched"  ->  "where do we take objects we never fill"
//
// Same word as `unikraft/alloc:untouched` and `unikraft/pages:untouched`, on
// purpose. `unused` reads as *finished with*, which is the state an object is in
// immediately BEFORE `give` and the wrong suggestion at a site whose meaning is
// "I never wrote here". A third spelling for one idea would be worse than a
// repeated one.
~pub tor untouched { obj: *Obj<!fresh> } -> *Obj<used!>

~proc untouched|zig {
    return obj;
}

// `uk_allocpool_return`. Accepts only `<!used>` and CONSUMES it, so returning
// the same object twice is a `Use-after-discharge` compile error — and a
// double-return is not a benign mistake here: `_prepend_free_obj` links the
// object into the free list a second time and increments `free_obj_count` past
// `obj_count`, which is the `UK_ASSERT` at pool.c:114 and is heap corruption
// without it.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_allocpool_return` returns void and cannot fail. Inventing
// one would be a lie about what the C does.
//
// The pool is BARE here, deliberately: `give` must be callable on a pool in
// either provenance, and its second argument is the one carrying the proof.
~pub tor give { pool: *Pool, obj: *Obj<!used> }

~proc give|zig {
    uk_allocpool_return(pool, @ptrCast(obj));
}

// ============================================================================
// END THE POOL
// ============================================================================

// `uk_allocpool_free`, gated on `<!owned>` — the provenance rule, lifted.
//
// **THIS CALL HALTS THE MACHINE, IN EVERY BUILD.** `uk_allocpool_free` reaches
// `UK_CRASH("Unregistering from `lib/ukalloc` not implemented.\n")` at
// pool.c:390 before it reaches `uk_free`, and `UK_CRASH` is not gated on
// `CONFIG_LIBUKDEBUG_ENABLE_ASSERT` (ukdebug crash.h:18-22). That is the C's
// state today, it is measured on a booted image in
// `tests/boot_free_crashes.kz`, and this tor neither hides it nor substitutes
// anything for it. `keep` is what a working program uses.
//
// It is bound anyway, and that is the point. The provenance rule is a rule
// ABOUT THIS FUNCTION; a lift that declined to bind it would be claiming to
// have lifted an assertion guarding a call it never makes. Here the assertion
// is dissolved for real: `<owned!>` is minted only by `create`, so
// `uk_allocpool_free` cannot receive a pool `uk_allocpool_init` built, in any
// program this module can express.
//
// Note what the two assertions below the gate become. pool.c:383 is retired by
// the state. pool.c:386 is retired by the object obligations, which are all
// discharged before this tor can be reached because `give` needs the pool and
// this tor consumes it. So by the time control reaches `uk_allocpool_free`,
// both of its assertions are already true by construction — and it still
// crashes, on the third line, for a reason neither assertion is about.
~pub tor free { pool: *Pool<!owned> }

~proc free|zig {
    uk_allocpool_free(pool);
}

// The pool lives for the life of the machine. No C call, and that is the honest
// emission: nothing happens, and the object file says nothing happens.
//
// This is not a convenience and not a loophole — in Unikraft today it is the
// ONLY way a pool can end, because `uk_allocpool_free` crashes (see `free`) and
// `lib/ukalloc` has no unregister for the entry every pool constructor adds to
// `_uk_alloc_head` (pool.c:388's own FIXME). A long-lived pool is also the
// normal shape: pools are built at boot, sized once, and used until the machine
// stops.
//
// It accepts BOTH provenances because the fact it records — "this pool is never
// released" — is true of both, and because it is what gives `<owned>` a second
// disposal so that DROPPING a pool is a diagnostic rather than a silent
// insertion. See the header note.
~pub tor keep { pool: *Pool<!owned|!hosted> }

~proc keep|zig {
    _ = pool;
}
